44a7a4da93a09510fb4372f8f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a/47660004af8c7eb8cb3de5f83042d3129092485a315696ee6f14551021a20f8e" "q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a/0dbbee0924d29cddc95e729e19738a391c447170486ca6319bb5fb581d916120" "q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a/ae17e2311ebdcb443976f856340f688b68e78591b3ccdd9c5d8ffe3ef1675120" "q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a/5f8e96fcef1cbe01e6835a0848fdcdb5b4ddc544a7a4da93a09510fb4372f8f7" "q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a/3eedad2b68f9e406204290c223fc7f33368e09cf74b8e7e09666a6fecaf84e6a" "q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a/590e223dd2b8bc7487dd03750c9d33f095cb4f45e3bb1a22990cb31f166d0d54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164506 :answer-field/correct 17592186164507 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-334307"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-2<0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable spiral (sink)}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334307/field-1/ma-v1/18e9b39a3a504d305bc6cd887976b1281c24aade77a4129d5b3b8c7a60f76e1a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1/5f8e96fcef1cbe01e6835a0848fdcdb5b4ddc544a7a4da93a09510fb4372f8f7" :answer/id #uuid "8f77cb9e-94a7-5355-b1a5-98a5929fdd15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1/590e223dd2b8bc7487dd03750c9d33f095cb4f45e3bb1a22990cb31f166d0d54" :answer/id #uuid "2ac2d9a1-0368-53ec-bebd-3829dc721124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1/ae17e2311ebdcb443976f856340f688b68e78591b3ccdd9c5d8ffe3ef1675120" :answer/id #uuid "e91ca916-c672-5607-b7f2-9730e35664bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1/3eedad2b68f9e406204290c223fc7f33368e09cf74b8e7e09666a6fecaf84e6a" :answer/id #uuid "29badc5f-bdce-5773-9a0e-a51beaf76345" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1/0dbbee0924d29cddc95e729e19738a391c447170486ca6319bb5fb581d916120" :answer/id #uuid "c6f54597-999c-5afd-9d2b-c9855633fde8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1/47660004af8c7eb8cb3de5f83042d3129092485a315696ee6f14551021a20f8e" :answer/id #uuid "33940d0a-80e6-56b8-a137-f3f2d1ae9944" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "92de6588-d4ed-534a-ad64-ab234c083ebc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334304/field-1/ae17e2311ebdcb443976f856340f688b68e78591b3ccdd9c5d8ffe3ef1675120" :answer-field/choices ["q-334304/field-1/5f8e96fcef1cbe01e6835a0848fdcdb5b4ddc544a7a4da93a09510fb4372f8f7" "q-334304/field-1/590e223dd2b8bc7487dd03750c9d33f095cb4f45e3bb1a22990cb31f166d0d54" "q-334304/field-1/ae17e2311ebdcb443976f856340f688b68e78591b3ccdd9c5d8ffe3ef1675120" "q-334304/field-1/3eedad2b68f9e406204290c223fc7f33368e09cf74b8e7e09666a6fecaf84e6a" "q-334304/field-1/0dbbee0924d29cddc95e729e19738a391c447170486ca6319bb5fb581d916120" "q-334304/field-1/47660004af8c7eb8cb3de5f83042d3129092485a315696ee6f14551021a20f8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-2/280edb51317f6a9c5b7147d2a4082131cdd45ac1307ffcecbd6e4226c2d1682b" :answer/id #uuid "3a1a99f8-b00f-5c75-8e12-592183231def" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a center" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-2/4912676f4da8ab51f553f74fb01abf9e1196a836c79544d0c8b6e6f56bf62370" :answer/id #uuid "8d31a58a-ea00-5b5a-92dd-e665b92719a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable spiral (source)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-2/0567ded365958d2b96c61c7991783dbda12d049410d79502fa82922588990c17" :answer/id #uuid "971403f8-b37d-5c44-841b-e997f46de756" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable spiral (sink)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4df8406c-12e4-5670-80b1-cf2f1bee18d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-334304/field-2/4912676f4da8ab51f553f74fb01abf9e1196a836c79544d0c8b6e6f56bf62370" :answer-field/choices ["q-334304/field-2/280edb51317f6a9c5b7147d2a4082131cdd45ac1307ffcecbd6e4226c2d1682b" "q-334304/field-2/4912676f4da8ab51f553f74fb01abf9e1196a836c79544d0c8b6e6f56bf62370" "q-334304/field-2/0567ded365958d2b96c61c7991783dbda12d049410d79502fa82922588990c17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334304" :question/id #uuid "3b03865d-7dc2-5a77-aba7-a0653e17d805" :question/math-academy-id "q-334304" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =4\\pm 7\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334304/field-1" "q-334304/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334304"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21657"] :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6feea8db62000e44334251a96c39bd8d0d8d7b1ec9a61380f3a5b4b613ea6c80.png)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/bceb494a6baeca8695157b4fd285b2880a242367747c0d72a0f573612ef87652.png)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21658"] :question/problem "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda = a \\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Provide an interpretation of the portrait and determine the possible values of $a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069757/assets/6d13d0dfadac45d40b74cb7e740a880305098f0939e2f45d74706c4022183c54.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21659"] :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda=\\pm2\\text{i}.$ Provide an interpretation of the equilibrium point (origin) for the system of differential equations given above." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
